LEVEL 5 → 6 — USING FIND WITH PROPERTY FILTERS


WHAT THIS LEVEL IS ABOUT : 

The password is hidden somewhere under the inhere directory across 20 subdirectories. It must meet three criteria: human-readable, exactly 1033 bytes in size, and not executable. This level introduces the find command — one of the most powerful file-search utilities in Linux.


APPROACH : 

I first tried the manual approach, checking each maybehere directory with cd and ls -la. This quickly demonstrated why an automated approach is necessary. The correct solution was:
find . -type f -size 1033c ! -executable
This returned a single result: ./maybehere07/.file2. I read it with:
cat ./maybehere07/.file2

Command Breakdown

Flag              Meaning
find .            Search from current directory
-type f           Match files only (not directories)
-size 1033c       Exactly 1033 bytes (c = bytes)
! -executable     Exclude executable files

Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]


KEY TAKEAWAY : 

find is indispensable for real-world tasks like locating misconfigured files, hunting for SUID binaries, or identifying recently modified files during an investigation.
